 "lines": [
  {
   "say": "Rich countries stopped having babies. Except one."
  },
  {
   "say": "Babies per woman, from nineteen sixty to today.",
   "screen": "Babies per woman, *1960* to *2024*",
   "at": "L1",
   "until": "L6"
  },
  {
   "say": "In nineteen sixty, Saudi Arabia, Iran, Syria and Jordan all had over seven."
  },
  {
   "say": "Europe, about two and a half."
  },
  {
   "say": "Then the big drop. Iran went from six to two in fifteen years."
  },
  {
   "say": "The Emirates, one point two. Italy and Spain, barely one."
  },
  {
   "say": "And Israel? It has stayed near three for thirty years."
  },
  {
   "say": "Today, only Iraq and the West Bank and Gaza are higher."
  },
  {
   "say": "And Israel is the only rich country near the top."
  },
  {
   "say": "Among thirty eight developed economies, Israel is number one."
  },
  {
   "say": "Why? Faith helps. But the Taub Center finds big families at every level of religion.",
   "screen": "Big families at *every* level of faith",
   "at": "L10",
   "until": "L10e"
  },
  {
   "say": "Even secular and traditional Israelis average over two kids."
  },
  {
   "say": "A rich, modern country that still bets on the future."
  },
  {
   "say": "So when someone tells you rich countries stopped having babies,"
  }
